{"source_key":"sha256-dcf513deed84d00d439c53236db67f5d","record_index":366,"record_hash":"sha256:6965ca0ab49ea705b31a40365c628ef44ff460fb0c727907167cdd6b5ead7fe6","timestamp":"2026-07-14T20:10:29.514Z","kind":"message","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:message","role":"assistant","text":"Yes—both pruning functions should be removable. They compensate for stack collection using the wrong boundary.\n\nToday every stack walks to one workspace-wide `lower_bound`, explicitly relying on later pruning ([init.rs]([REDACTED:path])). Different stack fork points therefore expose target ancestry.\n\nThe best replacement is:\n\n1. Compute the projection’s allowed segments with the existing `find_segments_reachable_from_a_not_b(ws_tip, target_commit, FirstParent::No)`, which already models `HEAD ^target_commit_id` ([api.rs]([REDACTED:path])).\n2. Pass that set only to projection stack collection. Reconciliation intentionally needs the existing raw lower-bound paths ([init.rs]([REDACTED:path])).\n3. Make it a hard traversal boundary—not another ordinary stop condition. Ordinary stops can be ignored for anonymous sibling segments and can restart collection as another stack segment ([init.rs]([REDACTED:path])).\n4. Check the starting segment too, because the current callback deliberately excludes it.\n5. Retain only a small presentation cleanup for empty metadata-backed branch shells.\n6. Delete `prune_integrated_segments`, `prune_integrated_stack_segments`, `commits_are_integrated`, and the post-prune base repair.\n\nStopping only at the target segment is insufficient. A stack can meet an ancestor of the target without traversing the target itself, especially with different fork points or a catch-up merge whose target is reached through the second parent. The existing regression at [with_workspace.rs]([REDACTED:path]) demonstrates this. `FirstParent::No` is required when calculating the revset; stack extraction itself remains first-parent/preferred-parent.\n\nI validated this in an isolated prototype:\n\n- 135/143 graph tests passed unchanged.\n- The stale-target, integrated-above-target, different-base, and catch-up-merge regressions passed.\n- Seven remaining failures were only changed stack-base labels—mostly correcting the old global/pre-prune base to the first omitted target ancestor.\n- One lost a stack ID previously inferred from a branch ref just below the boundary. Preserve that by letting stack-ID matching inspect the first omitted segment’s refs without including its commits.\n\nI would accept the corrected per-stack base changes and explicitly preserve that boundary ref as identity evidence. Recreating the old global base labels would reintroduce the compensating logic we are trying to delete.\n\nNo workspace files were changed; the prototype worktree was removed.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:691-704|note=[preserved target commit as the stable workspace anchor]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<rollout_ids>\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<[REDACTED:path]>","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-14T20:10:29.514Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"},"phase":"final_answer"}}}